WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.810
You hold a flame to cotton and the cotton burns.

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:11.140
You have seen it a thousand times — but have you ever actually seen the fire do the burning, or only one thing following the other?

3
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:18.790
Nine hundred years ago a scholar in Baghdad asked that question and pulled the ground out from under what we take to be science's firmest footing.

4
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:36.240
Bu kanal (`podcast-philosophy.json`) bir çeviri kanalı: sıfırdan içerik yazmak `kisit` alanını ("çeviri kaynağa sadık kalır") ihlal ederdi.

5
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:51.890
Bugünün bölümü `ceviri_uret.py` ile 05:39'da zaten üretilmiş — `felsefe.txt`'deki "Ateş Pamuğu Yakmaz" bölümünün çevirisi olarak `philosophy.txt`'de duruyor; taslaktaki 4 bölüm ve ton sırası (sakin, gerilim, kesif,

6
00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:57.940
sakin) birebir tutuyor, işaret satırları korunmuş, rakam ve Türkçe sızıntısı yok.

7
00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.070
İstenen anlatı metni (jenerik/teaser/kapanış hariç) şu:

8
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.600
You get up in the morning.

9
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.490
You walk into the kitchen, turn the knob on the stove, and a blue ring of flame rises up.

10
00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.020
You set the kettle on top of it.

11
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.670
A few minutes later, the water boils.

12
00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.320
At no point in this whole procedure do you stop to think.

13
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.650
You don't need to, because you know.

14
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.700
Fire heats water.

15
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.710
Hold your hand in a flame and it burns.

16
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.560
Let go of a glass and it falls.

17
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.570
Flip a switch and the lamp comes on.

18
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:41.900
Perhaps hundreds of decisions in your day rest on knowledge of this kind, and not one of them feels like knowledge; it feels like ground.

19
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.550
You don't question the existence of the thing you're walking on.

20
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.400
Now let's ask a very simple question.

21
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.250
How do you know that fire burns cotton?

22
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.580
The answer comes ready-made: I've seen it.

23
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.910
I've seen it a thousand times.

24
00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:03.480
Bring a lighter close to a pile of cotton and the cotton darkens, curls, catches.

25
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.170
Nobody disputes this.

26
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:10.140
But let's slow it down and ask what exactly it is that you saw.

27
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.990
What you saw was two events.

28
00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.840
First: the flame touches the cotton.

29
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.170
Second: the cotton burns.

30
00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:25.540
And between these two events there is a sequence — always in the same order, always close together in time.

31
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.590
That is what you saw.

32
00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.600
What you saw was an "after."

33
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:36.010
And yet you don't say "after." You say "because of." The cotton burned because of the fire.

34
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.540
The fire burned it.

35
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:44.270
There is a power in the fire called burning, and that power passed into the cotton and blackened it.

36
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.640
But did you see that power?

37
00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.050
You saw the flame, you felt the heat, you saw the smoke, you saw the blackening.

38
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:57.140
But a separate thing called "the power to burn" never appeared anywhere on the stage.

39
00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:02.070
It isn't something that happens in the scene; it's something you add to the scene.

40
00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.600
This may sound like word games.

41
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:10.810
But the consequences are very heavy, because almost everything we call knowledge is built on that addition.

42
00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:21.020
When a doctor prescribes a drug, when an engineer calculates that a beam will carry a load, when a mother tells her child not to touch the stove — all of them are taking the same step:

43
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.470
it has always gone this way until now, therefore it will go this way.

44
00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.320
Naming that step is not easy.

45
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.370
Is it an inference?

46
00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.260
A habit?

47
00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.950
An act of faith?

48
00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:42.280
Note carefully, the question is not "might fire fail to burn?" Nobody is inviting you to play with matches.

49
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:45.610
The question is what the word "cause" actually points to.

50
00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:50.380
There are two possibilities, and the two describe two very different worlds.

51
00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:55.150
The first possibility is this: cause is something out in the world.

52
00:03:55.150 --> 00:04:01.680
Combustibility lies in the very nature of fire, just as three-sidedness lies in the nature of a triangle.

53
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.010
If it didn't burn, it wouldn't be fire.

54
00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:11.060
And so it is necessary that fire burns cotton; anything else is logically impossible.

55
00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.190
The second possibility is this: cause is something in us.

56
00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.680
Out in the world there is only a regular sequence.

57
00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:24.410
A mind that lives through that sequence often enough begins to expect the second thing on the heels of the first.

58
00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:29.180
As the expectation hardens, it turns into a feeling of necessity.

59
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:37.470
Then we take that feeling and hand it back to the world, and we say "there is a power in fire." But the power is in us, in habit.

60
00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.960
We shouldn't underestimate the difference between these two.

61
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.330
In the first world, the laws of nature are etched into the fabric of reality and reason reads them off.

62
00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:54.020
In the second world, the laws of nature are a summary of our records so far, and they carry no guarantee for tomorrow.

63
00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:57.350
In the first, science discovers.

64
00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.800
In the second, science is a very meticulously kept betting ledger.

65
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.330
And this is not a modern argument.

66
00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:15.020
The man who posed the question in its sharpest, most unsettling form lived in the eleventh century, and the example he chose was precisely this one: fire and cotton.

67
00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:20.750
Al-Ghazali was born in ten fifty-eight, in the city of Tus in Khorasan.

68
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:30.320
He taught at the most illustrious school of his time, the Nizamiyya Madrasa in Baghdad; by his thirties he was one of the most celebrated teachers in the Islamic world.

69
00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:39.410
And facing him stood a new intellectual force: philosophers descended from the line of al-Farabi and Avicenna, who had rebuilt Aristotle in Arabic.

70
00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:43.220
That tradition read the world as a system of necessities.

71
00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:50.230
Everything had a nature, by that nature it produced particular effects, and reason could follow the chain all the way up to God.

72
00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:55.960
Al-Ghazali did not content himself with hurling abuse at this philosophy from the outside.

73
00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.410
First he learned it, and then he wrote a book summarizing what he had learned.

74
00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:05.020
Someone reading that book might well have thought its author was one of the philosophers.

75
00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:10.910
Then came the second book: the Tahafut al-Falasifa, The Incoherence of the Philosophers.

76
00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:18.560
His aim was to show that what the philosophers offered as proof was not proof at all, that most of it rested on groundless assumptions.

77
00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.370
The book takes up twenty questions one by one.

78
00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:28.420
One of them, the seventeenth, became one of the most fiercely debated pages in the history of philosophy.

79
00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:38.150
That section opens with this claim: the connection between what is habitually taken to be a cause and what is habitually taken to be its effect is not a necessary connection.

80
00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:40.520
Then comes the example.

81
00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.810
Cotton and fire are brought together, and the cotton burns.

82
00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:51.340
Al-Ghazali's question is this: does that scene prove that the thing doing the burning is the fire?

83
00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:53.870
No, he says.

84
00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:58.800
The scene proves only this much: the burning occurred together with the contact.

85
00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.130
"Together with" is not the same as "by".

86
00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:08.980
Observation gives you the first; the philosopher asserts the second; and between them lies a gap in the proof.

87
00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:11.830
So who is it that burns?

88
00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:20.920
The tradition of kalam within which al-Ghazali stood, and the Ash'arite line in particular, is clear on this question: the real agent is God.

89
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:27.450
The blackening of the cotton, the coming apart of its particles, the charring — God creates these directly.

90
00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:31.580
Fire is inanimate, and an inanimate thing cannot act.

91
00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:36.510
Fire is what happens to be present alongside the burning, not what does it.

92
00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:46.400
In this tradition the world is a fabric created anew at every instant; continuity comes not from any power belonging to matter, but from the unbroken repetition of creation.

93
00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:52.450
The objection that springs to mind here is entirely fair, and al-Ghazali raises it himself.

94
00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:57.540
If nothing is the necessary cause of anything, then anything whatsoever could happen.

95
00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.790
The book I left on the table as I went out, I might find as a horse when I return.

96
00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:05.480
A book could turn into a child.

97
00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:10.730
No plan can be made, nothing can be foreseen, reason goes bankrupt.

98
00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:14.380
Al-Ghazali's answer has two layers.

99
00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:22.350
The first layer: God has a custom, that is, God makes events flow within a settled order, and that order is not an arbitrary chaos.

100
00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:28.240
The second layer is subtler: God also creates in us the knowledge that this order will hold.

101
00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:36.050
So our certainty that the book has not become a horse comes not from a guarantee hidden in the nature of the book, but from a trust that has been given to us.

102
00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:38.580
In practice nothing changes.

103
00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:42.230
We make our decisions in exactly the same way.

104
00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:47.000
What changes is the name of the ground beneath those decisions.

105
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:51.130
And why al-Ghazali did this is plain.

106
00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:58.300
If the burning power of fire were a logical necessity, then not a single moment in which fire did not burn would be possible;

107
00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:10.750
and in that case the extraordinary events recounted in scripture — Abraham not burning in the fire, the staff turning into a serpent, the dead being raised — would be not merely unrealized, but unthinkable.

108
00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.080
Al-Ghazali did not want to close that door.

109
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:19.330
By breaking the philosophers' claim of necessity, he widened the field of the possible.

110
00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:24.580
But the price of what he did was very high, and there were those who noticed it immediately.

111
00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.670
Because the same move, while making room for miracles, also touched science.

112
00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:35.400
If the burning of fire does not come from fire, what is the point of studying fire?

113
00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.330
Can investigating nature be anything more than taking a photograph of a custom?

114
00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:48.140
The man who voiced this objection most powerfully was born fifteen years after al-Ghazali's death, at the other end of the Islamic world.

115
00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:51.790
Averroes was born in eleven twenty-six in Córdoba.

116
00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:57.520
He was a judge, a physician, and the most meticulous commentator on Aristotle.

117
00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:05.490
He wrote a line-by-line reply to al-Ghazali's book: Tahafut al-Tahafut, that is, The Incoherence of the Incoherence.

118
00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:12.340
His method is striking; he quotes al-Ghazali's sentences at length, then writes his own answer beneath them.

119
00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:15.190
It reads like the transcript of a debate.

120
00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:21.400
On the matter of fire and cotton, Averroes' counter-move is not theological but linguistic and logical.

121
00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:26.970
He says this: if burning does not belong to fire, then what does the word "fire" name?

122
00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:32.540
To know a thing is to know what it does, how it behaves, what nature is proper to it.

123
00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:41.950
Abolish natures, and what remains in your hands is a heap of events whose names resemble nothing; no word carries a fixed meaning, no definition can be made.

124
00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:50.400
For him, to deny causes is to deny reason, because what we call reason is precisely the capacity to grasp things together with their causes.

125
00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.570
Averroes' second point is more conciliatory.

126
00:10:53.570 --> 00:11:04.260
Fire burning cotton is not a single power working all on its own; the dryness of the cotton, the oxygen in the air, the duration of the contact, all of it is part of the business.

127
00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:10.630
What observation gives us, then, is not a simple "this did that", but an order of many conditions.

128
00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:19.560
Averroes did not give up seeing the ultimate source of that order in God; what he rejected was leaving nature without an agent in order to be able to count God as the agent.

129
00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:27.690
For him, secondary causes — that is, the effects things have of their own — are not a rival to God, but God's manner of working.

130
00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:31.820
The quarrel between these two great names locks here.

131
00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:38.350
Al-Ghazali says one cannot pass from observation to necessity, and he fills the gap with God's custom.

132
00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:44.720
Averroes says that if you accept that gap, knowledge itself will collapse, and he calls natures back.

133
00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:49.170
Both were saying something true, and the argument did not close.

134
00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:54.740
This book of Averroes' was translated into Latin at the beginning of the fourteenth century.

135
00:11:54.740 --> 00:12:03.350
The result was this: Europe read al-Ghazali's sentences about fire and cotton largely from the pen of the man who quoted them in order to refute them.

136
00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:10.360
It is one of the strange ironies in the history of thought; an objection traveled inside the very book that rejected it.

137
00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:13.530
Now let us leap six centuries forward.

138
00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:23.900
In eighteenth-century Scotland, a man not yet thirty years old, in a completely different world and for completely different reasons, is sitting on top of the very same gap.

139
00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:29.790
David Hume's concern was not miracles, not theology, not the agency of God.

140
00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:32.640
His concern was the human mind.

141
00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:44.610
In his great first book, A Treatise of Human Nature, and later in the more lucid Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, the question he asked was this: where does the idea of "cause" in our minds come from?

142
00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.460
His example is the billiard ball.

143
00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:51.910
One ball rolls, it strikes a second, the second moves.

144
00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:55.240
Let us put the scene under a microscope.

145
00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:56.970
What is there?

146
00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:59.340
The motion of the first ball.

147
00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.070
Contact.

148
00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:04.080
The motion of the second ball.

149
00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:07.410
Three events, one sequence.

150
00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.580
Now, which of these events is the "power"?

151
00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:12.150
None of them.

152
00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:14.520
You see the power nowhere.

153
00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:24.090
He gives another example: bread has nourished me before, but in the look of the bread, in its smell, in its colour, there is nothing that shows it will nourish me again.

154
00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:29.820
Turn the idea of bread over as much as you like — nourishment will never come out of it.

155
00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:35.870
Where Hume arrives is this: the only thing observation gives us is constant conjunction.

156
00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:46.560
As this conjunction repeats, a tendency forms in the mind; on seeing the first, it begins to expect the second — indeed, it becomes unable to stop itself from expecting.

157
00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:49.570
This inner compulsion he calls habit.

158
00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:54.820
The necessity is real, but it does not lie in the objects; it lies in us.

159
00:13:54.820 --> 00:14:00.550
And we project this inner feeling outward, onto the world, and say "fire has the power to burn."

160
00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:07.240
It matters to see that al-Ghazali and Hume are standing in the same place while looking in different directions.

161
00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:13.290
In the negative claim they agree: observation does not give us necessity.

162
00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:16.620
They part ways in what they use to fill the gap.

163
00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:20.110
For al-Ghazali, what is there is the custom of God.

164
00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:24.080
For Hume, what is there is the habit of human beings.

165
00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:35.730
There is no evidence whatsoever that Hume read al-Ghazali; he may have encountered an echo of these ideas through writers he is known to have read, such as Malebranche, but the resemblance is not a transmission.

166
00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:41.460
It is two people striking the same stone from two different places and finding the same crack.

167
00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:49.750
That crack was so unsettling that in seventeen eighty-three Kant wrote that the thing which woke him from his dogmatic slumber was precisely this objection of Hume's.

168
00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:58.360
Kant's solution was to give necessity neither to the world nor to habit; he positioned causality as a form the mind uses in constructing experience.

169
00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:02.330
The debate thus changed shape, but the gap did not close.

170
00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:07.420
If all of this sounds like a matter for the seminar room, let us now return to the kitchen.

171
00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:13.150
Because the mechanism Hume calls habit does not operate only in solid cases like fire and cotton.

172
00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:16.320
It operates always, in everything.

173
00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:19.330
And it does not discriminate.

174
00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:21.700
Think of a shirt.

175
00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:24.390
The day you wore it, your team won.

176
00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:27.880
You wore it a second time, and they won again.

177
00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:31.210
In the third week you chose that shirt deliberately.

178
00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:36.140
Even if you told no one, a small connection had formed inside you.

179
00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:38.830
The same mechanism.

180
00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:45.520
The succession of two events, the expectation, the expectation turning into a feeling of necessity.

181
00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:52.690
The mind does not ask "is this connection meaningful, or is it coincidence?" It looks at frequency and it forms a link.

182
00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:58.740
Between fire burning cotton and a lucky shirt, in terms of mechanism, there is no difference.

183
00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:05.910
The difference lies in the world behaving differently in those two cases; but the mechanism itself cannot see that from the inside.

184
00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.800
You had a backache.

185
00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.490
For a week it would not go away.

186
00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:17.340
On the fifth day you tried something: an ointment, an exercise, a mixture somebody recommended.

187
00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.030
On the sixth day the pain was gone.

188
00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:21.600
What do you say?

189
00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:31.970
"That worked." And yet most backaches, most colds, most passing complaints resolve on their own, and people start trying something at exactly the moment they feel worst.

190
00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:36.100
What comes after the worst moment is almost always a better moment.

191
00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:42.310
So even if the intervention does nothing at all, it still manages to land right alongside the recovery.

192
00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:48.840
That gap between "together with" and "because of" is skipped over, right here, millions of times, entirely unnoticed.

193
00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:53.290
There is a small experiment that shows how fundamental this tendency is.

194
00:16:53.290 --> 00:17:02.060
Skinner, in a study he published in nineteen forty-eight, put pigeons in a box and delivered food at regular intervals, no matter what the birds did.

195
00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:06.350
There was no real connection at all between the food and the behaviour.

196
00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:18.320
Even so, most of the birds developed their own peculiar rituals; one would swing its head in a particular direction, one would turn in circles inside the box, one would stretch its beak towards a certain corner.

197
00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:23.730
Whatever they happened to be doing just before the food arrived, they began to repeat.

198
00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:28.020
An empty connection turned into a belief purely because of timing.

199
00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:34.230
A good example of just how old, and just how crude, our tool for building causes really is.

200
00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:37.080
Now the final layer.

201
00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:45.850
Humanity could not close this gap, but it learned what to do with it, and today the institutions we trust most are built precisely around this gap.

202
00:17:45.850 --> 00:17:48.860
How do we know whether a drug actually works?

203
00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:54.910
Not by giving it to patients and watching them get better; that is just a more expensive version of the lucky shirt.

204
00:17:54.910 --> 00:18:04.000
Instead, we do something strange: we split people into two groups using the most meaningless method available to us, the logic of a coin toss.

205
00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:13.730
One group receives the drug, the other an imitation that cannot be told apart from it, and most of the time we make sure that neither the patient nor the doctor knows which is which.

206
00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.420
Why go to all this trouble?

207
00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:22.790
Because we now know that the sentence "I gave them this, and then they got better" proves nothing at all.

208
00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:27.720
What we call the control group is an institutional answer to Hume's objection.

209
00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:37.290
The necessity that observation refuses to give us, we try to force into being by constructing a world in which the only systematic difference is the intervention itself.

210
00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:44.620
A large part of modern statistics amounts to nothing more than writing the rules for when we are entitled to say "because of".

211
00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:54.030
The line "correlation is not causation" is the residue of an eleventh-century debate in Islamic theology, worn down until it fell into everyday speech.

212
00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:58.000
So the end of the story is not that someone won.

213
00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:05.810
The opening al-Ghazali pointed to was never closed; science did not get past it, it learned to work alongside it.

214
00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:12.340
The regularity of nature is still not a proven theorem today; it is a fact on which we build everything.

215
00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:19.030
And Averroes' warning still stands: without taking that order seriously, we can neither speak nor think.

216
00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:23.320
What remains is something practical, and a little unsettling.

217
00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:30.330
Habit is strongest where it has seen the most repetition; in cases like fire and cotton, that is exactly what saves us.

218
00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:36.540
But the very same mechanism can also produce a feeling of complete certainty after only three repetitions.

219
00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:40.510
The lucky shirt feels every bit as certain as fire.

220
00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:45.280
Which means our sense of certainty is not measuring the real amount of certainty at all.

221
00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:50.370
The only thing it measures is how many times we have seen two things side by side.

222
00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:55.460
The single antidote to this is catching that one small word in everyday life.

223
00:19:55.460 --> 00:20:01.190
When you describe something that happened, do you say "and then", or do you say "because of"?

224
00:20:01.190 --> 00:20:06.920
If it was the second, did you find that word out in the world, or did you add it yourself?

225
00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:15.370
Most of the time the answer is surprising, and that question is the most useful piece we still hold of the crack that fire and cotton opened a thousand years ago.

226
00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:17.420
---

